          "english_quote": "Bandini112 reports a Greek death verb in Lavra; this corroborates death wording without making the Latin supplement unanimous.",
          "issue": "The note adds a specific Greek-witness corroboration claim that is not substantiated by the supplied evidence excerpts. Those excerpts identify a bounded florilegium comparison record but do not supply its Bandini/Lavra reading, locator, or relevant qualification. This is an unsupported disclosure claim in this review packet, not a finding that the reported reading is false.",
          "suggested_revision": "Supply the attributed Bandini/Lavra report with its passage locator, reported Greek wording, and preservation or supplementation status, then review the corroboration claim against it. Alternatively, omit this sentence while retaining the disclosed working Latin reading and the distinction between editorial supply and preserved text.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "edition": "simplified",
      "verdict": "revise",
      "checks": {
        "complete_coverage": false,
        "no_added_claims": false,
        "names_and_negations": true,
        "speaker_attribution": true,
        "source_choices_and_notes": false,
        "terminology_policy": true
      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "si sic igitur tu doles de vestimento tuo, et quereris quod non illud integrum recipias, quid putas Dominum tibi facturum",
          "english_quote": "You grieve over your garment. You complain that you did not get it back whole. What, then, do you think the Lord will do to you?",
          "issue": "The conditional comparison becomes two factual assertions about the addressee. The source continues the hypothetical garment example with 'if, then, you grieve like this'; it does not assert that the addressee actually experienced the damaged garment. The simplified wording also loses the explicit comparative force of sic.",
          "suggested_revision": "Restore the conditional comparison, for example: 'If, then, you grieve like this over your garment and complain that you do not get it back whole, what do you think the Lord will do to you?' Review this proposed wording afresh before adoption.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "nonne igitur dominus spiritus eius propter hoc factum tuum [morte te] adficiet?",
          "english_quote": "Bandini112 reports a Greek death verb in Lavra; this corroborates death wording without making the Latin supplement unanimous.",
          "issue": "The simplified edition independently repeats a specific Greek-witness corroboration claim that the supplied excerpts do not substantiate. The referenced florilegium comparison itself is not included with the relevant Bandini/Lavra wording and locator. This evidentiary gap does not establish that the claim is false.",
          "suggested_revision": "Supply the attributed report, passage locator, reported Greek wording, and preservation or supplementation status for fresh review, or omit this sentence. Keep the disclosed bracketed Latin working reading rather than replacing it merely because alternatives exist.",
          "severity": "major"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "agite enim paenitentiam utilem vobis.",
          "english_quote": "Shorter sentences preserve the shepherd’s authority, ethical scope, blood-guilt and remedy language, and all noted alternatives.",
          "issue": "The simplification metadata claims preservation of 'blood-guilt' language, but this selected section contains no blood-guilt expression. The supplied source speaks of offenses, resentment, mercy, punishment, and beneficial repentance. The metadata imports a characterization unsupported by this unit.",
          "suggested_revision": "Remove the blood-guilt claim and describe only features present in this section, such as the garment–spirit comparison, resentment, mercy, punishment, and beneficial repentance. Any replacement metadata requires fresh review.",
          "severity": "minor"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Both editions require revision. The standard translation covers the selected Latin clauses faithfully, but its specific Bandini/Lavra corroboration claim needs supporting evidence or removal. The simplified edition additionally turns a conditional analogy into factual assertions and includes unsupported blood-guilt metadata. Both appropriately retain contextual repentance, speaker changes, negations, and the disclosed editorial death supplement. This review evaluates the supplied working source and reports; it does not authenticate unseen manuscripts or certify exhaustive collation."
}
